    text: 'Every afternoon during the long Indian summers, an old man named Kishan rode his bright orange ice-cream cart through the lanes of Sundarpur. His bell — a small brass bell with a wooden handle — would ring out clearly across the streets: tinkle-tinkle-tinkle.\n\nAt the sound of that bell, doors would fly open. Children rushed out clutching coins. Mothers stepped onto verandahs to wave. Even old Mr. Sharma, who was usually grumpy, would smile and ask for his favourite mango flavour.\n\nKishan knew every customer\'s name and preferred flavour. Little Riya always picked vanilla; the twins from House Number Seven shared a chocolate cone; and Mr. Sharma\'s mango bar had to be the last one in the bottom of the cooler — "the coldest," he insisted. Kishan made very little profit, but he often said his real reward was the joy on the children\'s faces. In a small way, that brass bell was the music of every summer evening.',
