    text: 'In the year 2157, eleven-year-old Margie lived in a world where books had long since been replaced by glowing screens, and schools were silent rooms in private houses. Her teacher was a large mechanical box installed in her bedroom — always patient, always strict, always exactly the same.\n\nOne afternoon, her friend Tommy found something astonishing in his attic. It was a real book — printed on actual paper, with pages that turned and a smell that seemed to belong to another world. The book described schools as they had existed centuries earlier: tall buildings where dozens of children gathered together each morning. There were classrooms with chalk, laughter in the corridors, lunch breaks shared with friends, and most amazingly, real human teachers — teachers who could joke, scold, encourage, and explain in their own special ways.\n\nMargie read in wonder. She could not imagine such a school. Her own "school" was a silent room with only the mechanical hum of her teacher for company. The screen displayed her lessons, marked her tests, and recorded her progress — all in cold, perfect efficiency. There was no whispering with friends behind a textbook, no shared lunch boxes, no group games at recess.\n\nThat evening, Margie sat through her usual arithmetic lesson. As fractions appeared on the glowing screen, her mind wandered. She thought about the children of the past — children who had learned together, laughed together, made mistakes together. She thought about how lucky they must have been. She thought, with a small ache in her heart, about all the fun they had had.',
